\par\par \b \uc2 SNAP ANALYSIS-Polish  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  may slow, not stop zloty gains\b0\par\par\uc2 431 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 April 2010\par 15:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Analysts see zloty up mid term on strong fundamentals\par \par \uc2 * Demand for Polish debt, state assets to underpin zloty\par \par \uc2 By Kuba Jaworowski\par \par \uc2 WARSAW, Apr 9 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will probably only slow, not stop, the zloty's gains given that Poland's economic fundamentals are relatively strong and investors are keen to buy into Polish assets.\par \par \uc2 Around midday on Friday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time since \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  let the zloty float freely ten years ago. The move prompted a 1 percent fall in the zloty which has recently hovered around 16-month highs against the euro, raising concerns that further appreciation could hurt the economy.\par \par \uc2 But analysts said Poland's status as the only economy in the 27-strong EU to avoid recession last year provided a solid base for zloty gains in the medium term.\par \par \uc2 "Today's move of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not... change Poland's image as an economy with strong fundamentals," Raiffeisen Bank Polska analysts wrote in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 "...that's why we expect the zloty to stay strong and the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only to weaken the appreciating move."\par \par \uc2 The zloty was trading around 3.876 per euro in late trade on Friday, after falling to an almost two-week low of 3.8907 earlier in the session. <EURPLN=>\par \par \uc2 BONDS, PRIVATISATION\par \par \uc2 Poland's economic growth is expected to accelerate to about 3 percent in 2010 from 1.7 percent a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say foreign investors are still lining up to buy zloty-denominated assets ranging from government debt to state assets. To do this they need to exchange their euros and dollars into zlotys.\par \par \uc2 "Factors supporting the zloty include demand for Polish bonds and the privatisation plans," Raiffeisen analysts wrote.\par \par \uc2 The total value of state assets on sale this year in the government's ambitious privatisation plan is seen at 25 billion zlotys. Poland's borrowing needs this year total 197 billion zlotys and the lion's share will be covered by local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds.\par \par \uc2 "We expect more noise on spot (...) but we remain confident in the medium-term uptrend in the zloty," Koon Chow, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  wrote in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 "We think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to slow the pace of zloty appreciation so as not to imperil the cautious cyclical recovery."\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Ruth Pitchford)\par \par \uc2 POLAND-ZLOTY/ (SNAP ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|PX|HX|CZS|RDN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100412e649000zd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
