\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Greek Woes, Obama-Klaus Talks On Radar\b0\par\par\uc2 By Margit Feher  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,694 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 April 2010\par 05:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par    Local/GMT \par  \par    0900/0700 Czech March CPI \par    0900/0700 Czech March jobless \par    0900/0700 Czech February industrial output \par    0920/0720 Bilateral meeting between US and Czech presidents, delegations \par    1000/0700 Russian Finance Minister Alexei Kudrin and other officials speak at the Association of Russian Banks conference in Moscow \par    1200-1600/0900-1300 Official construction launch for Nord Stream pipeline from Russia to Germany. Russian President Dmitry Medvedev and European gas and utility executives to speak \par    1200/0900 Kremlin economic aide Arkady Dvorkovich briefing about the upcoming BRIC economic summit in Brazil \par    1400/1100 Russian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chairman Sergei Ignatiev briefing on topics expected to include the end of rate cuts \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 While developments about Greece's funding crisis will continue to drive markets in Central and Eastern Europe, attention will also be drawn to Prague, where U.S. President Barack Obama and his Czech counterpart Vaclav Klaus meet for bilateral negotiations along with their teams. No specific outcome is expected, though a U.S. reassurance of its support for the Czech Republic is likely.\par \par \uc2 In the economic sphere, data scheduled for release are expected to show Czech annual inflation and the country's unemployment rate remained steady in March compared with the previous month.\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect Czech inflation to have remained unchanged at 0.6% on the year in March, though the figure should move higher later in the year, approaching the Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2% target, due to the base effect and a revival in the economy leading to increased demand-side pricing pressures.\par \par \uc2 Also, the March unemployment figure is seen hovering at 9.9%. While the country's economic growth remains meek at best, the jobless rate shouldn't rise because the unemployment rate usually falls in March as construction and other labor-intensive activities pick up, said Patrik Rozumbersky, an economist at \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  in Prague.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, "industrial output should drift higher, driven by Europe's industrial revival as fresh PMI [purchasing manager index] data suggest there's an acceleration in the industrial pickup," said Miroslav Plojhar, economist at \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 Analysts polled by Dow Jones Newswires forecast that output growth accelerated February, to 7.3% from a year earlier versus an annual growth rate of 5.3% in the previous month.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par         Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                    2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/CZK 25.1760-60 25.1880-80 -0.05  25.2200 25.1970  -4.60 \par EUR/HUF 267.67-17  267.80-29  -0.05  267.97  267.98   -1.07 \par EUR/PLN 3.8477-97  3.8435-80  +0.11  3.8482  3.8495   -6.28 \par USD/RUB 29.425-30  29.416-21  +0.03  29.426  29.395   -2.93 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Polish zloty is expected to remain range-bound and to trade within the PLN3.84-3.90 range against the euro, a local dealer said. That's despite attempts by politicians and central bankers to weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  via verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and hints that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to stop the zloty's appreciation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Czech koruna could weaken further Friday on the tails of the zloty amid Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Domestically, the Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will face fresh inflation and jobless data, though both sets of numbers are expected to be unchanged from the previous month, and are not expected to lead to any koruna volatility.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Hungarian forint will continue to be driven by developments in the Greek drama about that country's debt financing, a trader said. They noted that will probably remain the sole driver of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which has been surprisingly resilient to the Greek woes, as the upcoming parliamentary elections in Hungary are expected to bring a clear victory for the opposition party Fidesz.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Assuming a benign backdrop from crude prices, there is little the Russian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can do to halt the strengthening of the ruble against its dollar-euro basket, broker UralSib says. Still, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief Sergei Ignatiev may comment on the future of rate cuts at a meeting with journalists Friday. The market is curious to see if he agrees with Finance Minister Alexei Kudrin that Russian rate cuts should end because of the risk of resurgent inflation, the country's economic nemesis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par  \par HUNGARIAN BOND YIELDS--The three-year benchmark bond yield was at 5.90% Thursday afternoon from 5.84% late Wednesday. The five-year yield was 6.19% from 6.02%, and the 10-year was 6.80% from 6.72%. \par  \par POLISH BOND YIELDS--The average yield on two-year treasury bonds was 4.62% late Thursday compared with 4.53% late Wednesday, the five-year was 5.23% from 5.17%, and the 10-year was 5.68% compared with 5.59%. \par  \par CZECH BOND YIELD--The yield on the benchmark 10-year paper was 3.87% Thursday afternoon compared with 3.92% late Wednesday. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Polish Treasury bonds may continue their slide Friday following Thursday's corrective move, a local dealer said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Developments over Greece's funding crisis will set the mood for Hungarian government bonds Friday, rather than the first round of parliamentary elections in the country Sunday as the election outcome is regarded as "a done deal" with opinion polls placing opposition party Fidesz as the clear winner, a trader said. While Hungarian bond yields are no lower than those of Greece, the short end could continue to see a worsening if investor sentiment keeps deteriorating, he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par WIG20                    2522.36   loss   39.14   dn 1.5% \par BUX                     24480.67   loss  826.67   dn 3.3% \par PX                       1219.8    loss   23.4    dn 1.8% \par RTS                      1600.84   loss   13.0    dn 0.8% \par MICEX                    1476.59   loss    2.0    dn 0.1% \par Stoxx EU Enlarged 15    2,425.95   loss  52.20    dn 2.11% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN WARSAW: A sideways trend is expected in the immediate future, eventually leading to a downward correction in the WIG20 index to 2,350 points as large foreign investors continue emptying their portfolios, boosted by gains from the strong zloty, a trader said. Some selling is partly in preparation for the large IPO of insurer PZU expected in May, the trader added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN PRAGUE: Czech stocks will remain driven by sentiment on core European markets but will also seek direction from the U.S., local traders said. In general, Czech blue chips may face more profit-taking Friday after posting gains earlier this week. CEZ and NWR, closing down 1.6% and 1.8% on the day Thursday, respectively, may also face further sell offs Friday, the traders added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN BUDAPEST: Hungarian stocks have been rising sharply this year so far, thus any negative news about Greece will trigger a downward correction, a trader said. Domestic news, such as the upcoming general elections, are on the back burner and are not expected to affect sentiment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN MOSCOW: Moscow equity markets will probably have a "boring" end to the week, with little scope for a correction or rally after the Micex and RTS sank Thursday on a decline in oil and worse-than-expected economic statistics from Europe and the U.S. Shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gazprom\f28 \uc2 \f2  may be active as European energy executives and President Dmitry Medvedev gather to officially launch construction of the Nord Stream pipeline to Germany. [ 04-08-10 2306ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  OTHER NEWS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: The presidents of the U.S. and Russia, meeting here to sign a major arms-control treaty Thursday, also began negotiating a specific package of sanctions against Iran that President Barack Obama confidently predicted would win United Nations passage this spring.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: The Russian oil giant Lukoil decided to stop oil supply to Iran so as to avoid possible loss caused by U.S. sanctions against the country, the Russian business daily RBC said Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: The net profit of Russian alcoholic beverage producer Synergy, also known as Sinergiya, decreased 10% on the year to 1.148 million rubles in 2009, the company said in a statement Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: French wine and spirits company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pernod Ricard SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  (RI.FR) and privately held \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russian Standard\f28 \uc2 \f2  Vodka Thursday said they have settled their U.S litigation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Poland's entire natural-gas industry and a special U.S. envoy in charge of energy gathered at a shale gas conference Thursday sponsored by the likes of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chevron\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Exxon Mobil\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Halliburton. U.S. giants will start drilling for shale gas in Poland within the next few weeks. If they find meaningful amounts of it, Poland's energy mix, carbon-dioxide issues and even external policy may change completely.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Poland has sold a Japanese private sector entity carbon dioxide emission quotas worth EUR30 million, Poland's environment minister said.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 POLAND\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The National Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is prepared for an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to prevent excessive strengthening of the zloty that could hamper economic growth, a management board member of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: The Czech Republic's total debt rose 22% in January through March to 1.22 trillion koruna ($64.37 billion), compared with CZK1 trillion in the year-earlier quarter, the finance ministry's quarterly Debt Report showed Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Hungary's ruling Socialists filed a complaint against the right-wing opposition Fidesz party Thursday for illegal use of personal data ahead of general elections that kick off this weekend.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Hungary's 2010 budget deficit target can be achieved if the country's next government is committed to the task following parliamentary elections, which start Sunday, state news agency MTI quoted Finance Minister Peter Oszko as saying Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: A Romanian supreme court judge was detained Thursday on suspicion of corruption, the first time that such a senior member of the judiciary had been arrested, judicial sources said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Margit Feher, Dow Jones Newswires, +36-20-925-2364;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 margit.feher@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Malgorzata Halaba in Warsaw, Sean Carney and Leos Rousek in Prague, and William Mauldin in Moscow contributed to this briefing.) [ 04-08-10 2306ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100409e64900027\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
