\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Poland \highlight\uc2  May  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  If Zloty Rises More - Citi\b0\par\par\uc2 1,377 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2010\par 11:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0947 GMT [Dow Jones] The Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is not happy with the rapid strengthening of the zloty and may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market on a higher scale than expected if the Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rises further against core \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says Citi economist Piotr Kalisz. Says the new MPC is taking a more active approach to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy than the previous one whose term ended in February, but markets should pay attention to any comments by the head of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  who has the power to authorize \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Adds the government already has substantial amounts of the euro and is unlikely to buy more on the market. EUR/PLN 3.8506 from 3.8252 late Wednesday. (marcin.sobczyk@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0935 GMT [Dow Jones] It looks like yen bears may need to go back into hibernation for a while as a general wave of nerves boosts the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Steve Barrow at Standard Bank reckons JPY is a buy from here vs USD and also vs CHF and NZD. Targets USD/JPY at 89.90. Now at 92.90 - the low for the day, and the month, so far. (katie.martin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0929 GMT [Dow Jones] Most Thai government bonds higher as foreigners buying to park money after sharp fall in Thai bourse today. "Foreign players are very active and they put money in government bonds' benchmark tenors," says bond dealer; adds further gains in government bond prices likely as heightening political tensions after government declares state of emergency in Bangkok, surrounding areas force investors to flee into safe haven assets. Yields expected to go down by 5 bps Friday. Bid/offer yields of issue due March 2013 quoted at 2.70%/2.65% vs 2.72%/2.70% late yesterday, May 2015 at 3.43%/3.40% vs 3.48%/3.46%, November 2016 at 3.60%/3.55% vs 3.60%/3.58%, June 2019 at 3.80%/3.75% vs 3.83%/3.81%. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0926 GMT [Dow Jones] India interest rate swaps remain weighed as market already positioned for upmove in rates, leaving little headroom for fresh bets, says trader with foreign bank; 1-year OIS at 4.95%/4.98%, unchanged from previous close, 5-year OIS at 6.89%/6.92% vs 6.88%/6.92% last close. "The market had been so heavily positioned for monetary tightening from the RBI that there's isn't much of a move now, even though we have inflation data just around the corner," says trader. Adds, traders now focussing on building steepening positions on growing concerns government borrowing program may prompt spike in yields. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0925 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan exports in March aided by strong demand from China, also as rising crude oil prices boosting exports of petrochemicals, says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  economist Cheng Cheng-mount. "First-quarter (exports) growth is very positive and there's no question this is an upside risk" to 1Q GDP, says Cheng, adds \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, will likely maintain low-rate environment as domestic demand remains weak, unemployment high. "It's too early to raise rates," he says. Taiwan March exports +50.1% on-year to US$23.37 billion, beating +43.32% forecast by 11 economists surveyed by Dow Jones, increasing for fifth straight month, highest since August 2008's record US$25.21 billion. (alex.pevzner@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0922 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds extend losses as traders bet on "a slow death" for current benchmark, says trader with primary dealership; 6.35% 2020 bond at 89.95 vs 90.30 last close. "Traders fear that the benchmark is going to be illiquid after the next and probably last auction (Friday) for the bond," says trader. Tips 10-year note to slip to 89.75 in session.(khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0916 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CZK trades up at 25.220-25.250 from 25.18 late Wednesday largely on renewed concerns over Greece and the stability of Greek banks. "Greek woes pressure the euro and also its periphery \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the koruna," a local trader says. Czech March CPI and jobless data due early Friday may provide some floor for the koruna, the trader adds. (leos.rousek@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0915 GMT [Dow Jones] The sell-off in Greek government bonds makes it increasingly likely that Greece will have to change strategy and ask for a rescue, which it will get from the IMF and its European partners, says Laurent Bilke, senior Europe economist at Nomura. "We continue to exclude default or restructuring as a potential scenario," Bilke says. As well as the rise in bond yields, it is the volatility that could stop Greece returning to the bond market. "When two-year yields move by 150bps intraday to close 100bps higher as they did Tuesday, and rise further by about 50bps the following day, it is likely to be very difficult for any bond issuance to take place." (mark.brown@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0915 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS steady after pricing in PBOC's 3-year bond sale. 1-year unchanged at 2.24%-2.31%, 5-year flat at 3.71%-3.79%. Trader at Shanghai-based foreign bank says market soon digested impact from PBOC bond sale given its limited scale, not high yield. "The swaps have priced in expectations of two to three interest rate hikes, so they will remain rangebound in the near term." PBOC sells CNY15 billion worth of 3-year bonds at 2.75% in open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  today, 1st such sale since June 26, 2008. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0915 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are little changed after data show euro zone retail sales in February fell at their sharpest pace since December 2008. Retail sales fell 0.6% on month, while analysts forecast no change over January. June bund is up 41 bps on the day at 123.52 while the benchmark 10-year bund is up 30 bps at 101.40. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0913 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR higher at 9,070 vs 9,050 late yesterday as local players cover USD short positions after pair's slide to lows not seen since early August 2008, dealers say; "I expect the dollar to trade within IDR9,040-9,080 tomorrow as foreigners seem keen to buy Indonesian sovereign bonds," dealer says; referring to further rally in bonds, which send benchmark 10-year yield to fresh 3-year low of 8.94%. On other hand, foreigners also take profit on Indonesian shares, which may prevent pair from sliding beyond 9,040; Bank Indonesia deputy governor Sarwono tells Dow Jones Wednesday \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  believes IDR's current rise not a matter for concern, but further strength may erode competitiveness of country's exporters, especially if gains are quick. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0910 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia shares end lower, snapping 12-day winning streak, benchmark KLCI down 0.9% at 1332.93 with across-the-board losses led by property (down 1.4%), construction (down 1.1%) plays. Local dealer says profit taking amid weak regional sentiment "not a surprise" after recent strong gains; adds, "weaker-than-expected February industrial output data is also causing some concern" but notes \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Zeti saying Thursday GDP forecast of around 5% has further upside potential, giving "a little reassurance." Tips market to continue to consolidate tomorrow, mainly in 1330-1340 band. 438 decliners vs 219 advancers today; decliners include Gamuda (5398.KU) down 4.3% at MYR2.86, CIMB (1023.KU) down 3.0% at MYR14.20, Kencana (5122.KU) down 3.1% at MYR1.59, UEM Land (5148.KU) down 2.7% at MYR1.42, AirAsia (5099.KU) down 3.6% at MYR1.35. Advancers include Affin (5185.KU) +1.6% at MYR3.25, BIMB (5258.KU) +3.6% at MYR1.44, APM (5015.KU) +2.2% at MYR4.20. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com) [ 04-08-10 0547ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100408e6480007g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
