\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Swiss Jobless Drop Reflects Robust Econ Upswing\b0\par\par\uc2 1,230 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2010\par 09:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0733 GMT [Dow Jones] The drop in Switzerland's unemployment rate last month confirms the economy is moving in the right direction, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "It is a very good sign when a lagging indicator like unemployment is improving," says economist Reto Huenerwadel. The rate fell to 4.2% in March, from 4.4% a month earlier. "The decline in youth unemployment and the number of short-time workers also suggest the economic recovery is fairly broad-based," he notes. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0732 GMT [Dow Jones] India's WPI-based inflation may peak in March and drift lower in coming months as upticks in weekly price readings led by specific seasonal trends rather than an across the board rise, says Anubhuti Sahay, economist at Standard Chartered Bank. "The food price rise was led by items like fish and marine products, and fruits and vegetables, which could correct from current levels," says Sahay. Adds, while spillover from primary price pressures into manufactured articles remains likely, move may be offset by easing of prices in other components of index such as sugar. Expects March inflation to print at 10.18%. Inflation data due April 15. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0727 GMT [Dow Jones] April HSI futures +0.1% at 21,911 vs cash market down 0.1%. Contract trading at single-digit premium to cash now, vs April fair value about 30-point discount to cash, continuing to flag positive near-term bias, suggesting cash market's pullback merely due to mild profit-taking following 5.5% rally in past 6 sessions. Volume modest at 48,791 lots. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0727 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds end up despite PBOC's resumption of 3-year bond sale, as move has little impact on existing ample liquidity, says Beijing-based analyst with local insurer; notes PBOC will sell 3-year bonds once every 2 weeks, compared with weekly issue frequency on 1-year, 3-month bills in regular OMOs; lower issue frequency also indicates PBOC's efforts to soothe market concerns over imminent interest rate hike. Shanghai exchange government bond index ends at 124.63 vs 124.27 last close, may hover around current levels in coming sessions. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0727 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR continues to weaken Thursday, largely driven by Greek concerns and now all attention is on the ECB press conference at 1230 GMT for any clues on extension of the collateral mechanism, which could aid Greece and halt the EUR's slide. Comments on liquidity normalization will also be closely watched. EUR/USD hit 1.3306 in Asia, its lowest level since March 25, and is now at 1.3322. EUR/JPY also lower despite weak machinery orders data in Japan and trades near a two-week low of 124.13. EUR/GBP is up a tad at 0.8768 while EUR/CHF is barely changed at 1.4327. Thursday's data include German industrial production for February due 1000 GMT, euro zone retail trade figures are out 0900 GMT. UK February industrial production data are due 0830 GMT while the BOE rate decision comes out at 1100 GMT. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0726 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD rangebound in quiet trade as investors remain cautious before talk between U.S. Treasury Secretary Geithner and Chinese Vice Premier Wang Qishan later today, says trader at Chinese bank. Pair last at 7.7621 vs 7.7617 this morning, 7.7640 yesterday; tips 7.7600-7.7630 band today. "However, the upcoming IPO of Agricultural Bank of China may draw demand for local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the middle of this year," trader says. Agricultural Bank of China plans to conduct IPO in both Shanghai and Hong Kong in 3Q or early 4Q, people familiar with situation told Dow Jones Newswires. The planned IPO may help the bank to raise around US$20 billion worth of capital. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0722 GMT [Dow Jones] RBA will likely continue raising interest rates sharply through 2010 and into 2011, moving beyond its stated aim of restoring policy to a more normal setting as the country's economic recovery gathers pace. Cash rate expectations have crept higher, partly on the back of the concerns about rising house prices and the strong commodity price upturn. "Normal" rates may be as little as 50 basis points away, with analysts starting to think a cash rate of 5% by year end could be conservative. Noted RBA watchers like \uc2 Macquarie Bank\uc2  interest rate strategist Rory Robertson now on track for 6.5% by end 2011, a target that will afford the RBA little time to pause. "RBA sooner or later almost certainly will find what it sees as good reasons why policy normalization is not enough," Robertson says; it will find "reasons to start pushing toward a more restrictive stance for policy." See "=FOCUS: RBA Likely To Limit Pause And Push Policy Toward Restrictive Setting" (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0721 GMT [Dow Jones] GBP could be a good buy on dips, suggests \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 . It notes cable's inability to fall below $1.5130 in spite of the soft UK CIPS PMI, and the poor 5-year gilt auction, hint that any cable dip is a buying opportunity again. It suggests the GBP will make a break for $1.5325 if today's IP figures come in strong. The GBP is now at $1.5170. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0718 GMT [Dow Jones] RBA will deliver another hike in May or June, meaning AUD/USD will climb higher than expected, says Westpac Chief Economist Bill Evans. Retains year-end forecast of 0.9500, but now looks for pair to trade at 0.9400 in June, 0.9400 in September, both revised higher. Says plenty of risks remain, but overall outlook is positive. "Our central view however, now precludes any major shift in sentiment regards risk and hence a repeat of the sharp 5 cents sell off in the AUD in January/February." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0716 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the zloty, Citi says, seeing high risk of such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Recent comments by \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance ministry representatives and central bankers show growing concern at PLN appreciation. Citi says: "Since the ministry, following recent bond issues, has significant euro reserves, the move is on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's side." (malgorzata.halaba@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0715 GMT [Dow Jones] If EUR/USD doesn't recover to 1.34 fast then expect the pair to continue "sinking in quicksand," says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 , with 1.33 and 1.3267 the next targets. The pair is now at 1.3322. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0715 GMT [Dow Jones] Beijing will soon have music for bears' ears. JPY and EUR bears, that is, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Focus by Nicholas Hastings. After months of speculation, the People's Bank of China appears to be on the verge of releasing its CNY peg against the dollar. This will probably allow the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fluctuate in a wider daily band, much as it did between 2005 and 2008. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com) [ 04-08-10 0333ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100408e6480005r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
