\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Poland  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  May Weaken PLN - Citi\b0\par\par\uc2 1,277 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2010\par 09:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0716 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the zloty, Citi says, seeing high risk of such \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Recent comments by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance ministry representatives and central bankers show growing concern at PLN appreciation. Citi says: "Since the ministry, following recent bond issues, has significant euro reserves, the move is on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's side." (malgorzata.halaba@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0715 GMT [Dow Jones] If EUR/USD doesn't recover to 1.34 fast then expect the pair to continue "sinking in quicksand," says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 , with 1.33 and 1.3267 the next targets. The pair is now at 1.3322. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0715 GMT [Dow Jones] Beijing will soon have music for bears' ears. JPY and EUR bears, that is, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Focus by Nicholas Hastings. After months of speculation, the People's Bank of China appears to be on the verge of releasing its CNY peg against the dollar. This will probably allow the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fluctuate in a wider daily band, much as it did between 2005 and 2008. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0713 GMT [Dow Jones] iTraxx Japan Series 13 widens to 95 bps vs Wednesday's 92 bps as stronger JPY blurring outlook for Japan's export-driven economy, dragging down Tokyo shares, prodding investors to buy back protection against bankruptcy risks, says Akira Nomura, credit analyst at Mizuho Securities. But adds, "the iTraxx is unlikely to continue rising as many players have become more optimistic about the Japanese economy than previously. Corporate profits have been improving, so investors are not in a position where they keep buying back CDS actively." No significant single-name CDS deals done on Thursday. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0712 GMT [Dow Jones] The drop in the Swiss March unemployment rate is a surprise, and mirrors renewed business confidence amongst industrial companies, says \uc2 Zuercher Kantonalbank\uc2 . The Swiss jobless rate slipped to 4.2% from 4.4% in February, according to the latest data. "The rate of decline was surprisingly steep, and is positive news for the economy," says economist David Marmet. "The level of industrial orders is giving companies the confidence to take on workers, and that's boosting job creation," he adds. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0706 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI down 1.0% at 1332.78 with property (down 1.6%), construction (off 1.4%) stocks leading losses on profit-taking after strong rally in recent days, says dealer. Adds, "concerns over economic recovery may start to creep in," following weaker-than-expected February industrial output data. "Although economists have not yet revised their GDP forecasts, the second set of weaker-then-expected data (February export numbers also came in below view) is bound to cause some concerns... data for March will be watched with particular interest to determine if this is becoming a trend," says dealer. Tips immediate support at 1329 (April 2 low). Market breadth negative with 477 decliners vs 140 advancers. Decliners include IJM (3336.KU), down 2.5% at MYR4.68, CIMB (1023.KU), down 2.6% at MYR14.26, Supermax (7106.KU) down 2.1% at MYR7.29. Advancers are Tan Chong (4405.KU), +2.5% at MYR4.11, Sunway (6289.KU) +2.4% at MYR3.79. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0701 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD still lower at 31.583 vs 31.628 yesterday, marking 5th straight session of declines, but local bank trader says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to buy USD "heavier than it recently did" in late session, as regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' advances seem to have slowed. Notes stronger TWD vs regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may hurt Taiwan's export competitiveness. Tips pair in 31.540-31.620 band rest of session; "the pair is not likely to fall below key 31.500 support in short term," as further upside for stock market may be limited after Taiex adds 8.4% since beginning of March, touches highs not seen since January, he says. Foreign fund inflows to stocks may be tapering off, with NT$3.5 billion today vs yesterday's NT$11.39 billion after 8 straight sessions of net buying by foreign investors; Taiex ends down 0.8%. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0658 GMT [Dow Jones] India interbank call rate almost flat as banks flush with ample cash allocated to government projects released into banking system, says dealer with state-run bank; overnight call rate on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's negotiated dealing system at 3.52% vs 3.50% at Wednesday's close, CBLO at 3.00% vs 2.75% at open. "Banks are flush with liquidity because of year-end deposits from corporates and up to INR320 billion-INR450 billion of unspent funds being released by the government today," dealer says. Total INR31.51 billion of call money trades reported so far vs about INR80 billion-INR100 billion usually. Lenders borrowed under CBLO window to lend to RBI at higher rates, says dealer. Tips call rates may hover around 3.00% during session, CBLO may fluctuate between 3.50% to 3.75%. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0656 GMT [Dow Jones] There is now increasing uncertainty surrounding Greece's ability to raise the required amount of funding without recourse to the emergency lending facility, says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  strategist Steven Mansell, who calculates the funding need at EUR11.6 billion by end-May. "We think that we are getting ever closer to this point, particularly if the [Greek Public Debt Management Agency] PDMA struggles to find strong sponsorship for its global dollar-denominated issue...," he says. As the terms of activating this emergency funding are still very unclear, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  sees little scope for market tensions to dissipate in the near term. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0655 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds slip tad after sequential uptick in primary price data raising fresh concerns on potential \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightening, says trader with primary dealership; 6.35% 2020 bond at 90.20 vs 90.23 before data, 90.30 last close. "This is a bit of a concern, but for now, the potential illiquidity of the benchmark is the main worry for the market," says trader. Tips bonds to remain under pressure, trade in 90.00-90.40 band for session. Primary articles price index rose 0.6% to 285.1 in the week to March 27.(bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0653 GMT [Dow Jones] June bunds are higher Thursday, extending gains from Wednesday, but the contract needs to break its previous high of 123.78 for the bullish impetus to continue, says Sean Maloney, rate strategist at Nomura. Contract trades up 37 bps at 123.48. Resistance pegged at 123.52, 123.78 and 124.0, while support levels are at 123.04, 122.78 122.53. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0653 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY falls as investors trigger stop-loss selling orders around 124.00 using Greek debt concerns as excuse, say senior dealers at major banks in Tokyo. Cross may fall to 123.50, where good amount of stop-loss orders are placed vs last 124.01. "As Greek bond yields spiked over the past couple of days, investors are increasingly worried that the nation might default for real, and that's hurting the euro," says \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2  senior dealer Yuichiro Harada. EUR/USD last at 1.3313, may fall to 1.3250, next technical-chart key level, dealers say. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com) [ 04-08-10 0316ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100408e6480005f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
