\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Glencore CDS Moving Tighter After Convertible\b0\par\par\uc2 1,001 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Januar 2010\par 12:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1122 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Glencore International\f28 \uc2 \f2  5-year CDS moving tighter, now 18 bps better on the day at 116/122 bps, according to Markit. The company recently boosted its capital base by selling $2.2B of convertible bonds can be converted into Glencore shares upon an IPO. (MWI)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1116 GMT [Dow Jones] Commerbank forecasts emerging market debt issuance to exceed $50B in 1Q of this year. Adds, Vietnam, Indonesia and Philippines are expected to be first out of the blocks with new bonds likely in the coming weeks. (CAC)\par \par \uc2 1059 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  expects euro-zone governments to front-load their 2010 funding requirements and forecasts EUR25 billion of syndicated bond issues in January. This sum includes EUR7 billion from Spain, EUR4 billion from Belgium and EUR4 billion from Austria, all in 10-year bonds, strategists Nishay Patel and Steven Mansell say. They also envisage EUR5 billion from both Greece and Ireland in five-year bonds. "We expect non-core issuers to complete a greater proportion of their projected funding requirements in January this year than in January 2009," they add. (BEM)\par \par \uc2 1055 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  says that bullish government bond curve steepening will remain the main theme in Hungary in 2010. Expects the 2s5s segment of the curve to steepen the most as 5-year/5-year rates have been coming off due to resurfacing shy expectations of EUR adoption. "This in turn makes 2s5s10s payer positions fundamentally attractive." Also expects 1-year/1-year rates to move toward 6% as markets realize the substantial negative output gap won't allow inflation to spike anytime soon. (MAF)\par \par \uc2 1053 GMT [Dow Jones] Despite current consolidation, the EUR/USD is still under the bearish threat of extending the dominant weak tone below 1.4218 towards the key 1.4000/1.4045 support cluster. See Monday's Charting Europe column by Francis Bray. (BFR)\par \par \uc2 1046 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds rise slightly after chief economic adviser Kaushik Basu says no expectation of monetary tightening now, inflation to peter out in few months; 6.35% 2020 bond at 91.11 vs 90.97 earlier, dealer at foreign bank tips 90.95-91.30 band for rest of session. "Overall, domestic demand/supply balance is also positive but global data--like China's PMI--is weighing on bonds everywhere. From the monetary policy perspective, the market has factored in more than what the RBI is likely to do," dealer says. (HAR)\par \par \uc2 1045 GMT [Dow Jones] Turkey's 5-year CDS trades over 60bps above Brazil, "which we believe might be too wide... especially if the IMF deal materializes," says Luis Costa of \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . Adds, implementation of a new IMF agreement will likely to benefit Turkey in terms of fiscal frameworks. "If that is actually achieved, investors should expect another overshooting on Turkey external debt risk, what can easily push Turkey 5-year CDS towards the 150bp level," says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 's Costa. (CAC)\par \par \uc2 1044 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD unwound oversold momentum signals over the holiday period however the absence of evidence for a sustainable recovery suggests its rebound was a correction in an ongoing downtrend says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . A trading range below 1.46 this month would be viewed as bearish says the bank with any daily close below the 200 DMA at 1.4225 opening risk toward 1.4065 and possibly 1.3735 before signs that the longer-term uptrend is resuming. EUR/USD now at 1.4380. (GST)\par \par \uc2 1032 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR forward premium down, tracking weakness in spot pair, 1-year at 1.25/1.27 vs 1.30/1.32 late Friday; "exporters have been receiving, seeing the weakness in the spot dollar/rupee. The receiving has been particularly strong in the quarter ending months," says dealer with state-run bank. USD/INR currently at 46.31. (SDS)\par \par \uc2 1024 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PKR higher at 84.57 vs 84.25 late Thursday on higher importer demand to meet payments. "The dollar rose sharply as banks had to arrange around $100 million for oil companies to clear their amount against imports of furnance oil," says dealer. Adds, "the dollar is likely to inch up further as about $30 million more is due against oil imports." Pair tipped in 84.50-84.65 band Tuesday, slight downward pressure due only from intermittent exporter selling, dealer says. (HAZ)\par \par \uc2 1023 GMT [Dow Jones] The exchange rate of the Polish zloty versus the Czech koruna is seen going up in the next few months as the macroeconomic and policy divergence grows bigger in the two countries, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . Adds CZK has significantly outperformed the PLN since mid-2008, losing 8% versus the euro, while PLN is around 25% weaker. Notes concerns over the strength of the Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Adds \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ambitious privatization plan is likely to generate significant capital inflows, supporting the zloty. PLN/CZK now at 6.4370. (MMS)\par \par \uc2 1019 GMT [Dow Jones] Minutes from the Riksbank's Dec. 15 rate-setting meeting were slightly on the soft side, but with few surprises, says Handelsbanken. Says the two hawks differ on the timing of the first rate hike rather than the rate level, with Lars Nyberg coming out as a touch softer than Barbro Wickman-Parak. Svante Oberg also appears a touch softer than previously, saying he could see a need to increase the repo rate more slowly than the Riksbank's current prediction. It expects to begin raising the key rate from a record-low 0.25% in autumn 2010. Sweden's two-year bond yields trade six basis points higher at 1.76% while the ten-year yield is up four basis points at 3.43%. (ANM)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 04, 2010 06:23 ET (11:23 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100104e614001ny\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
