\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Polish CPI, Current Acct Data Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 By Margit Feher   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,397 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2009\par 05:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par Local/GMT \par  \par 1000/0900  Czech January current account \par 1400/1300  Polish January current account \par 1400/1300  Polish January foreign trade \par 1400/1300  Polish February CPI \par 1400/1300  Polish revised January CPI \par 1400/1300  Polish February M3 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BUDAPEST (Dow Jones)--Poland is releasing a flurry of economic indicators Friday, of which February consumer prices and revised January inflation figures will likely be the most important.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The finance ministry has already predicted an uptick in annual inflation to 3.4% in February, mostly due to the depreciating zloty and regulated price hikes for residential utilities. But analysts' forecasts are all over the map, ranging from 2.9% to 3.6%.\par \par \uc2 A high-end figure would likely give an extra nudge to the rallying zloty but not necessarily keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from easing interest rates later this month, as the rate-setting Monetary Policy Council has already flagged its readiness to ignore any inflation uptick as a strictly short-term blip.\par \par \uc2 Poland's January current account data will also be watched, especially for how well foreign direct investment is holding up as both exports and imports hit the skids due to the euro zone's deepening recession.\par \par \uc2 The Polish zloty and the Hungarian forint may benefit further Friday from the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move Thursday to weaken the Swiss franc.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a blessing for \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Hungary, where pressure on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  derived from a serious \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mismatch - many mortgages in recent years were based on Swiss francs, said analyst Marc Chandler of BBH. The recent strength of the Swiss franc compounded those countries' woes.\par \par \uc2 "Today's dramatic weakening of the Swiss franc may ease this pressure," Chandler said. Nevertheless, he retained a negative outlook for the region, saying "the macroeconomic situation is not supportive."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might ease the roll-over costs of Swiss francs for some CEE banks and, if sustained, might have a positive impact on the retail banks' non-performing loan levels, said \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  analyst Martin Blum.\par \par \uc2 Blum also agreed, however, that the SNB's move likely "won't take out the general bearish trend from the CEE \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par         Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                    2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/CZK 26.315-65  26.345-85  -0.11  26.410  26.355   -1.64 \par EUR/HUF 295.35-65  294.38-67  +0.33  295.35  295.08  +12.07 \par EUR/PLN 4.4843-93  4.4885-25  -0.09  Untraded today   +8.97 \par USD/RUB 34.974-80  34.995-05  -0.06  35.180  34.962  +14.57 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Hungarian forint is likely to gain Friday on the Hungarian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's announcement Thursday that it will start converting EUR2.4 billion in E.U. support funds on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market in the course of this year. The SNB's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the franc market may also lend some short-term support.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Polish zloty was off late Thursday on another of its periodic bear-market rallies after the SNB's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the Swiss franc, but \f28 \uc2 \f2 Toronto Dominion Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist Bartosz Pawlowski said the Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's firming trend won't be broken until the euro/zloty dives below its 50-day moving average, currently at 4.48-4.49. Pawlowski said he is pessimistic on the chances of such a move but added that any such breakout would encourage more aggressive zloty exposure from offshore investors. The euro/zloty was 4.5420 late Thursday, versus 4.6365 early Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Czech koruna is seen staying stable Friday or shedding some its gains against the euro. There may be some profit-taking following the koruna's recent firming, a local trader said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite some ruble weakness Thursday, analysts still see more of a chance that the Russian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prevent a ruble rise, rather than a fall. "The market awaits the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to start \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  soon as a significant ruble strengthening will be negative for the real economy; a weak ruble provides competitive advantages to local exporters, a very important factor as demand and commodity prices continue to be low," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The Russian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakened Thursday to RUB39.59 from RUB39.31 against a basket of dollars and euros due to lower oil prices and a small drop in international reserves.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par  \par HUNGARIAN BOND YIELDS - The three-year bond yield was at 13.30% late Thursday from 13.35% late Wednesday; five-year was unchanged at 12.65%; 10-year was at 11.40% from 11.35%. \par  \par POLISH BOND YIELDS - The two-year average yield was at 5.64% late Thursday from 5.73% late Wednesday; five-year at 5.96% from 6.05%; 10-year was at 6.14% from 6.21%. \par  \par CZECH BOND YIELD - 10-year benchmark was 4.92% late Thursday from 4.91% late Wednesday. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungarian government bonds clawed back their morning losses by late Thursday to finish essentially unchanged as the forint managed to dip below the 300 level versus the euro. "Sentiment is now more positive, the Swiss \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's measures also help emerging markets," a trader said. The Hungarian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's announcement on starting the conversion of about EUR2.4 billion in E.U. support funds on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market in the course of this year should also support the forint and bonds in the days ahead. The trader saw a chance for treasury bond yields to decline further Friday, especially if U.S. markets hold on to their current gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Polish Treasury bonds were stronger late Thursday, helped by sharp gains on the zloty. "Zloty rally outweighed concerns that the budget deficit may need to be increased," a local trader said. If the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  extends its gains Friday, treasuries will follow suit, he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par WIG20                    1494.19  loss    6.76  dn 0.5% \par BUX                      9461.29   loss  274.64  dn 2.8% \par PX                         677.3   gain    1.7   up 0.3% \par RTS                       618.93   loss    8.67  dn 1.4% \par DJ Stoxx EU Enlarged 15: 1244.62   gain    6.68  up 0.5% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN WARSAW: Stocks closed Thursday down while the market recovered most of its midday losses helped by better-than-expected U.S. retail sales data and a rally of the Polish zloty. Sentiment remained moderately positive and if the U.S. market ends the week at late Thursday's levels, the WIG20 may head toward 1600 next week, a local fund manager said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN PRAGUE: Stocks ended Thursday mixed amid thin trade due to a thin corporate news flow, a trader said. "In Prague and in the region most stocks are trading lower, and Friday we may see investors close short positions," the trader added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN BUDAPEST: Prices, except for some blue chips, didn't move much Thursday, a Raiffeisen Bank trader said. OTP's slow downward trend will likely continue although there are no big sellers in the stock any more, and even strong support at HUF1,500 could be broken. Investors would welcome any OTP-related news, the trader added. MOL will be watched to see whether the rising oil price trend of the last week lends support to the stock. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Magyar Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Thursday drop was a surprise, not supported by stock's fundamentals. Stock prices in Budapest will likely continue to erode Friday although a positive U.S. close would raise hopes for a rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN MOSCOW: Russia investors will mainly focus on news Friday ahead of Sunday's Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries meeting, which could see a cut in oil output. Traders said the market could see a rally Friday if the oil price increases further. "Where oil trades over the next few days will be very important for sentiment toward Russia," said UralSib. Stock prices were pulled lower Thursday by negative sentiment on Norway's Telenor getting its shares in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vimpelcom\f28 \uc2 \f2  frozen by a Russian court. Both the Russian Trading System and the ruble-denominated MICEX index gained some lost ground later in Thursday's session on positive U.S. macroeconomic figures and rising oil prices. "The feeling is that we're past the bottom," said Troika's Igor Prokhayev. [ 13-03-09 0421GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090313e53d00040\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
