\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 4- \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  rally on forint rise; leu up after aid talks\b0\par\par\uc2 1,155 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2009\par 17:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Forint, stocks rally lead \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher, aid bonds\par \par \uc2 * Hungary c.bank talks up forint, may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Leu edges up as Romania closer to IMF deal\par \par \uc2 (Recasts with forint, adds bonds, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Marius Zaharia and Jason Hovet\par \par \uc2 BUCHAREST/PRAGUE, March 10 (Reuters) - The forint led gains in central Europe on Tuesday after Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it was actively supporting the forint and Romania sought aid to ride out the global crisis.\par \par \uc2 The forint jumped more than 2 percent over Monday levels in the late afternoon, pushing the Polish zloty higher and the Czech crown -- the region's best performer this year -- to a two-month high as a rally in stocks added to gains.\par \par \uc2 Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Andras Simor signalled in a TV interview that the bank was actively supporting the forint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though he would not say how and in what amounts the bank had bought forints.[ID:nLA544310]\par \par \uc2 The pledge followed a weekend statement saying the bank would channel European Union funds into the market and pump euro liquidity into the banking system to prop up the forint, which sank to a record low of 317.45 to the euro last week.\par \par \uc2 The Hungarian forint <EURHUF=> firmed 1.9 percent to 304.3 to the euro by 1602 GMT, but has lost 13.9 percent this year, outpacing up to 12 percent losses for the region.\par \par \uc2 "It's hard to say anything, I can only make guesses," a local dealer said, giving a 70 percent probability the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had bought forints. "We haven't seen the (\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) and we saw interest from hedge funds to buy the forint."\par \par \uc2 In Poland, the zloty <EURPLN=> gained 1.4 percent and the Czech crown <EURCZK=> rose 1.7 percent, helping lift bond prices, dealers said. The leu <EURRON=> gained just 0.3 percent to 4.287 to the euro, with dealers saying only the size of any IMF-led aid deal was likely to benefit the Romanian unit.\par \par \uc2 Central European stocks jumped, riding a boost in global sentiment after news banking giant \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  was profitable to start 2009. [ID:nLA118651]\par \par \uc2 Policymakers have become more vocal in support of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that have dropped sharply in 2009 as the global economic slowdown punishes central Europe's export-heavy economies.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed to prop up the zloty. Last month it sold EU funds on the market when the zloty neared a record low.\par \par \uc2 ON THE GROUND\par \par \uc2 Capital flows to emerging Europe that once fuelled booming growth have reversed, putting strain on many countries. In Tuesday note, \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  analysts estimated the region would need combined gross external financing of $460 billion in 2009.\par \par \uc2 Romania said on Tuesday it had started talks with the European Commission, the International Monetary Fund and other institutions to seek "medium-term foreign financial assistance", a bailout which economists said could reach 20 billion euros.\par \par \uc2 "The leu would have firmed on the IMF news, had it depreciated in line with the region in the past," one dealer said, adding news on the size of the deal could be a mover.\par \par \uc2 "The market is pricing in 5-10 billion euros, but if (Romania) gets 20 billion euros, the leu should rally."\par \par \uc2 Dealers have said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold euros in the market in this year, helping the unit fare better than peers.\par \par \uc2 An IMF team was due to start a two-week visit to Bucharest on Wednesday to discuss a potential loan programme. President Traian Basescu told Parliament on Monday a foreign loan was the only solution to aid the troubled economy. [ID:nL9487165]\par \par \uc2 "The package should ease fears of a complete financial meltdown in Romania," Neil Shearing, emerging European economist at Capital Economics, said in a note.\par \par \uc2 "More generally, an IMF rescue package does not alter the fact that the leu remains fundamentally overvalued."\par \par \uc2 Hungary secured a $25 billion IMF-EU package in October, becoming the first EU member to get aid. Analysts have speculated for weeks that Romania would become the third bloc member -- after Hungary and Latvia -- to get help.\par \par \uc2 Unlike Latvia, Romania has not experienced large street protests but analysts believe social pressures will continue to mount, threatening the new government coalition's stability.\par \par \uc2 Policymakers have repeatedly stated over the past weeks that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were undervalued, and stronger countries like the Czech Republic and Poland have tried distancing themselves from neighbours' problems. But analysts predict further \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakening.\par \par \uc2 "The previous flow of capital is turning into an ebb which is fundamentally what will maintain pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Swedish bank SEB's chief emerging markets strategist Mats Olausson said.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ----------------------MARKET SNAPSHOT-------------------------\par \f28 \b \hig