\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Stocks lower, zloty weakens further\b0\par\par\uc2 677 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2009\par 13:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Emerging equities broadly weaker led by Asia\par \par \uc2 * Regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are mixed, zloty lower\par \par \uc2 * Latvian lat remains above \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level\par \par \uc2 * Ukraine assets little hit by ratings downgrade\par \par \uc2 By Martina Fuchs\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 26 (Reuters) - Emerging equities were broadly lower on Thursday, led down by Asian stocks and underperforming global markets, with \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  raising the spectre of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the zloty \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened again after Wednesday's rate cut.\par \par \uc2 Benchmark emerging equities <.MSCIEF> fell 0.27 percent by 1145 GMT, after a 1 percent rise the previous day, but remain down 11.11 percent on the year -- still above multi-year lows set late last year.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, the benchmark global equity markets index <.MIWD00000PUS> was up 0.15 percent supported by fresh steps from the British government to shore up its banking sector.\par \par \uc2 Much of the emerging fall was down to Asian stocks, with Chinese equities <.SSEC> down 3.87 percent. Israeli stocks <.TA25> lost 0.32 percent, but some of the other exchanges in the region were up.\par \par \uc2 Hungarian equities <.BUX> were amongst the largest gainers, up 0.76 percent but still down 18.62 percent on the year, while Czech stocks <.PX> were up 0.55 percent.\par \par \uc2 "We've had a slight rebound in the last couple of days, but now we are back and worries have not gone away," said \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  chief emerging markets analyst Lars Christensen. "There is a little weakness in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but it's relatively neutral."\par \par \uc2 Benchmark emerging sovereign debt spreads <11EMJ> were five basis points narrower at 648 over US Treasuries, implying a slight rise in appetite for emerging debt compared to US government paper.\par \par \uc2 Two of the region's worst performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- Hungary's forint <EURHUF=> and Russia's rouble <RUS=MCX=> -- were slightly stronger, up 0.51 and 0.17 percent respectively but both remain down around 13 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 POLISH \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 The Czech crown <EURCZK=> was up 0.49 percent, but the Polish zloty <EURPLN=> lost 0.1 percent to deepen its recent losses, but remained above all time lows set earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut interest rates by 25 basis points on Wednesday, saying a sharp slowdown in the economy justified the cut. Poland has now cut rates by 200 basis points in recent months to 4.0 percent, although Wednesday's cut was not as sharp as previous ones.\par \par \uc2 Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Slawomir Skrzypek said on Thursday that the bank might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the zloty. [ID:nWAR007969]\par \par \uc2 The Latvian lat <EURLVL=> was slightly weaker within its trading band but remained short of Wednesday's intraday lows, when it fell sharply to the level where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support it.\par \par \uc2 A government collapse, ratings downgrade and accelerating economic crisis have increased pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it could get short-term support in the coming days as Latvia receives the first part of a 7.5 billion euro International Monetary Fund and the European Union-led package.\par \par \uc2 The cost of protecting Ukranian sovereign debt in the credit default swaps market fell 124.5 basis points to 4162.5, meaning it would cost $4.16 million a year to cover $10 million of five-year debt -- amongst the highest in emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  slashed Ukraine to a CCC+ "junk" rating on worries about destabilised banks, falling industrial output and political disunity that puts at risk a $16.4 billion International Monetary Fund loan programme. [ID:nLP372975]\par \par \uc2 "The two-notch ratings downgrade appears premature in terms of timing and too aggressive in terms of magnitude," said Sergei Voloboev, emerging markets analyst at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . "There was no significant market reaction. It seems that the market has not paid too much attention to the arguments that the rating agency made yesterday. But of course, Ukraine continues to trade extremely wide." (editing by Peter Apps ad Toby Chopra)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-EMERGING|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|CZS|PX|HX|RDN|GNN|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090301e52q00097\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
