\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: CEE Equity Story In The Spotlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sean Carney   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,704 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2009\par 05:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par Local/GMT \par  \par N/A Russia: U.S.-listed CTC Media (CTCM), one of Russia's largest broadcasters, reports fourth-quarter results before U.S. markets open. Presser at 0900 GMT in Moscow. \par N/A Poland: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Telekomunikacja Polska SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  (TPS.WA), \f28 \uc2 \f2 PKN Orlen\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PKN.WA), and \f28 \uc2 \f2 PKO Bank Polski\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PKO.WA) all report fourth-quarter earnings before local market opens at 0730 GMT. \par 0000 Thurs/2300 Wed: Czech petrochemical group \f28 \uc2 \f2 Unipetrol AS\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BAAUNIPE.PR) publishes 2008 results. \par 1030/0930: Unipetrol press conference. \par 0000 Fri/2300 Thurs: Hungary's oil group MOL Nyrt (MOL.BU) releases fourth-quarter earnings. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 PRAGUE (Dow Jones)--Investors will take a breather from focusing on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility Thursday and turn their focus to regional stocks, namely oil companies which report fourth-quarter and 2008 earnings.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Czech \uc2 Unipetrol AS\uc2  is expected to post a sharp annual widening of its fourth-quarter net loss due to lower revaluation of inventory caused by falling crude oil prices in the period.\par \par \uc2 The drastic drop in oil prices is seen as the main culprit behind an expected loss also at Hungary's MOL Nyrt, which will also have a huge negative impact on inventory valuations.\par \par \uc2 The weak forint and option conversions will also take a toll on MOL's financials, traders said.\par \par \uc2 At both companies' press conferences, investors will be listening for news on measures how management will tackle the effects of recession, as well as operating environment guidance.\par \par \uc2 In Warsaw Thursday, the focus will be on stock market reactions to overnight fourth-quarter earnings releases by Poland's biggest blue chips - \uc2 Telekomunikacja Polska SA\uc2 , \uc2 PKN Orlen\uc2 , and \uc2 PKO Bank Polski\uc2 , all of which will report before the Warsaw Stock Exchange opens at 0730 GMT.\par \par \uc2 In addition, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will release the full text of its latest inflation report, including updated inflation and GDP projections for 2009-2011, on its Web site at 1100 GMT.\par \par \uc2 In Moscow, CTC is the country's major company to publish financial results for the period. The results are expected to reflect major advertising cutbacks by cashless consumer goods companies.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par         Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                    2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/CZK 28.513-33  28.560-14  -0.16  28.582  28.528   +6.58 \par EUR/HUF 302.90-20  301.80-00  +0.36  302.90  301.91  +14.93 \par EUR/PLN 4.6982-02  4.6905-25  +0.16  4.6997  4.6917  +14.17 \par USD/RUB 35.796-02  35.825-51  -0.08  35.831  35.680  +17.27 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro remains trapped in between CZK28.2 and CZK28.4 in thin trade Thursday as the market is getting mixed signals, a trader said. Some investors and analysts are panicking and lumping the koruna in with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  like the leu and forint, while others are giving an ear to the Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  which says the Czech fundamentals remain strong.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If U.S. data turn out to be favorable this week, the euro/forint could touch support at HUF293.80 and a range-trade could start between HUF293.80 and HUF298.82, CE Broker said. If data happen to be negative, the euro could break resistance of HUF298.82 and touch HUF305 again, it added. Raiffeisen Bank adds that uncertainty over the euro/forint will persist, volatility and levels of around HUF300 will remain with a significant strengthening in the forint against the euro expected to only come in the second half.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Poland's zloty Thursday will be at the mercy of investors digesting Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  comments late Wednesday hinting that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will now pause in cutting rates to observe economic trends and zloty rate. Wednesday the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gave up most of its earlier gains despite the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  saying it may use "direct" actions to support the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that it considers undervalued, suggesting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is possible.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Weakening pressures on the ruble look set to continue easing Thursday on the back of a higher demand for ruble liquidity to pay month-end taxes. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  closed Wednesday at RUB40.29 against the dollar-euro basket, from RUB40.43 Tuesday. Traders see the ruble remaining stable for the rest of the week, provided there is no sharp downturn in oil prices - Russia's chief export.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par  \par HUNGARIAN BOND YIELDS - The three-year bond yield was at 13.3% late Wednesday from 13.5% late Tuesday; five-year was at 12.40% from 12.45%; 10-year was at 11.50% from 11.65%. \par  \par POLISH BOND YIELDS - The two-year average yield was unchanged on the day at 5.62% late Wednesday; 5-year flat at 5.85% from 5.81%; 10-year at 6.12% from 6.10%. \par  \par CZECH BOND YIELD - 10-year benchmark at 4.87% late Wednesday from 4.84% late Tuesday. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trade of Hungarian government bonds Thursday is expected again to be thin with direction depending to a large extent on the forint, a trader said. Wednesday, local bonds clawed back some of their Tuesday losses amid very shallow volumes. "The prime minister's (misinterpreted) comments were the main reason behind Tuesday's weakening and we saw a minor correction of this today," a trader said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Polish Treasury bonds are seeing little movement in recent days and will remain in thin trade as investors remain on the sidelines until they get the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's latest inflation projection, a trader said. The current projection shows 2009 CPI ranging at 2.5%-3.9%, above the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's 2.5% target, indicating that the monetary policy board may be less eager than thought to cut rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par WIG20                    1345.2    loss   15.9   dn 1.2% \par BUX                      9886.91   loss  429.6   dn 4.2% \par PX                        642.1    loss    2.8   dn 0.4% \par RTS                       538.1    gain   13.4   up 2.6% \par DJ Stoxx EU Enlarged 15: 1156.11 loss 23.31 dn 2% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN WARSAW: The outlook for local stocks Thursday remains bearish, local analysts said, citing the withering of early euphoria Wednesday after the market opened strong. The slightly weaker zloty is also expected to keep some investors at bay. Wednesday, \f28 \uc2 \f2 PKN Orlen\f28 \uc2 \f2  closed down 3.6% ahead of the company's late-night earnings release. Pekao down 1.3%; Lotos up 5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN PRAGUE: Unipetrol is likely to open lower Thursday after investors Wednesday showed they have little faith in the petrochemical company; it to close down 4.4%. Analysts expect Unipetrol's FY08 net loss to have almost doubled annually; results out very early Thursday. CEZ keeps its relative buoyancy Wednesday thanks to news it was part of a group buying a German coal mine to fuel its power plants. Other stocks trade mixed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN BUDAPEST: Selling pressure is likely to persist this week but "the bottom could be close," a local trader said. Wednesday, there was a powerful selloff in OTP shares, and the bank ended down 6.7%. "Negative news from Ukraine and a Merrill target price cut kept OTP under heavy pressure and other stocks assisted to the decline," the trader said. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Magyar Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2  down 4.7%, MOL 3.2% lower in highly volatile trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN MOSCOW: Shallow trade is seen Thursday as the market waits for U.S. investors to choose a direction, a trader said. Prices late Wednesday were almost unchanged from opening prices, as the market remains in thrall to the U.S., where stocks and crude oil reversed recent losses. \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  trader Maxim Gulevich said few people want to sell stocks with prices so low. Oil stocks are the strongest performers as crude oil hovers around $40 a barrel, chiefly \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gazprom\f28 \uc2 \f2  up 1.0%, Rosneft 2.9% higher. [ 02-25-09 2300ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  OTHER NEWS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Poland's Treasury Minister Aleksander Grad told Dow Jones Newswires Wednesday a plan to privatize the Warsaw Stock Exchange has been accelerated and is due to be completed this year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: The Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Monetary Policy Council Wednesday supported government efforts to defend the zloty, explicitly reminding markets that direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets remains an option for the bank.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 POLAND\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \uc2 \f2 PKN Orlen\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PKN.WA) Thursday said it swung to a fourth-quarter net loss, reflecting massive charges due to depreciation of oil inventories and high financial costs of foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debt, and said these factors would affect results in 2009.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: Russia's new budget that predicts the country's first budget deficit in a decade still needs further concessions to allow for the increase in anti-crisis spending, the country's finance minister said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: Russian automaker IzhAvto said Wednesday it had offered to buy back 2 billion rubles worth of its bonds on August 26.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Hungary can give its companies up to EUR500,000 of financial aid until the end of 2010, to help them deal with the economic crisis, the European Commission said Wednesday. The commission also approved state aid in the form of reduced interest rates on loans concluded by the end of 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Hungary's financial market authority, PSZAF, will make general provisioning mandatory for all domestic banks, even those which could theoretically ask for an exemption due to their strong capital position, the PSZAF said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: The Romanian Finance Ministry plans to sell 7.5 billion lei ($2.25 billion) of state treasuries in six auctions in March, news agency Mediafax reports bank dealers as saying Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: Romania's economy won't grow in the first quarter of 2009, compared with the same period a year earlier, Prime Minister Emil Boc said Wednesday, news agency Mediafax reported.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BULGARIA: Bulgaria's parliament debated a new no confidence motion against the centre-left government Wednesday over its "total failure" to meet its election pledges, but the measure was almost sure to fail.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Sean Carney, Dow Jones Newswires; +420 222 191 375, sean.carney@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Margit Feher and Edith Balazs in Budapest, David McQuaid and Marek Strzelecki in Warsaw, William Bland and Lidia Kelly in Moscow contributed to the briefing.) [ 02-25-09 2300ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020090226e52q0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
