\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: USD/CAD Shifts Lower As Stocks Improve\b0\par\par\uc2 1,060 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2009\par 18:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York. Darlene Ross, 201 938-2085; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1700 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CAD shifted somewhat lower as US stocks gained back some lost ground while remaining well in negative territory. A substantial advance in crude oil futures may also be helping CAD, although the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's correlation with oil and other commodities has been weaker in recent weeks. USD is currently around C$1.2560, but trading remains choppy. USD's high for the session is C$1.2608, according to EBS. (DBC)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1648 GMT [Dow Jones] Poland's zloty was clearly undervalued when it weakened close to PLN5 to the euro, central banker Jan Czekaj said in remarks to the press Wed. Poland's Monetary Policy Council defended the zloty, explicitly reminding markets that direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is possible. \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's PM hopes to resolve its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  problems by adopting the euro. Prime Minister Tusk said Wed recently that he expects the ECB to be flexible on the terms of entry into the European Union's exchange rate mechanism, or ERM 2. (RFO)\par \par \uc2 1647 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude oil imports to the US have fallen 13% over the past four weeks to average 8.8 million barrels a day last week, according to official US government data. This probably contributed to the smaller-than-expected increase in crude inventories. What we're finally seeing is the pledged output cuts by OPEC, which were expected to take hold this month, catching up to the new reality of slack oil demand. Further declines in crude imports could be in the offing. April Nymex crude +1.8% at $40.67 a barrel. (ANR)\par \par \uc2 1633 GMT [Dow Jones] Euro has recovered from its session lows vs dollar and yen as stocks come off earlier lows. Trading is very volatile, swerving with changes in risk appetite. Wed morning in NY, EUR/USD was at 1.2747 from 1.2850 late Tue, while USD/JPY was at 97.45 from 96.80, according to EBS. EUR/JPY was at 124.24 from 124.38. DJIA down 127. (RFO)\par \par \uc2 1622 GMT [Dow Jones] Polish Treasury bonds are little moved following MPC comments after the earlier 25bp rate cut. "The market is waiting for details of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflation projection," a local economist says. She adds the projection now showing 2009 CPI range at 2.5%-3.9%, above 2.5% target, may indicate that MPC may be less eager to cut rates than thought so far. The 2-year paper at 5.62% unchanged from Tuesday; 5-year at 5.85% from 5.81%; 10-year at 6.12% from 6.10%. EUR/PLN at 4.6975 from 4.6220 in early trade; USD/PLN at 3.6860 from 3.5950. (MES)\par \par \uc2 1617 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys have ticked lower with $32B 5-yr note auction looming, despite weaker stocks and poor housing data. Data showed existing homes sales fell to nearly 12-yr low in Jan. Tsy market losses heaviest in 5-yr and 10-yr sectors; 5-yr yield up by nearly 4 points to 1.92%, 10-yr up more than 4 points to 2.84%. 2-yr note steady at 1.03%. DJIA down by over 2%. Auction results to come at 1 p.m. ET. (DLB)\par \par \uc2 1606 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Freddie Mac\uc2  (FRE) posted a "new record" for month-over-month increase in delinquencies for January, says \uc2 Barclays\uc2  analyst Bruce Harting. Total delinquencies rose 26 bps to 1.98% in Jan. The increase "is probably at least partly due to the foreclosure moratorium, which prevents delinquent loans that don't cure from leaving the delinquent loan population through foreclosure as new delinquencies continue to build," Harting says in a note. "It appears that not only are we not near a stabilization point, but that the pace of deterioration continues to hold." (ASA)\par \par \uc2 1604 GMT [Dow Jones] Ukraine's debt sinks further into the red after S&P's downgrade sends credit deeper into junk territory. Risk premium on \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's EMBIG 40 bps wider at 3383 bps over Tsys, returns a negative 1.78%. That compares to broader EM spread 4 bps tighter at 709 bps over Tsys, returning a negative 0.14%. Ukraine's CDS trading at 3350-3365 running points range, according to CMA Vision and Markit; both not seeing points upfront - a measure of heightened levels of distress as it carries an annual $500,000 fee on top of expense - yet. (CSS)\par \par \uc2 1559 GMT [Dow Jones] Turkish bonds and the lira are weaker. Testing 1.6810 earlier, USD/TRY trades at 1.6930 from 1.6910 Tuesday. Meanwhile, the benchmark November 3, 2010 bond also losses ground but still remains stronger compared to Tuesday. The benchmark issue ends trade at 15.10% from 15.21% Tuesday and it moves to 15.18% in Thursday value-dated transactions. Analysts expect TRY to trade between 1.6850 and 1.7000, and the benchmark bond between 15.1% and 15.3%. (IHA)\par \par \uc2 1556 GMT [Dow Jones] Bernanke is rather confident the Fed's current programs will not create an inflation problem later, and is sanguine that what the Fed has done in the current crisis can be unwound in an orderly fashion. (MSD)\par \par \uc2 1554 GMT [Dow Jones] House Financial Services Chairman Barney Frank says Fed's longer-term inflation forecasts aren't a target. Frank called the new, longer-term forecasts a "thoughtful advancement" but "this is not inflation targeting." (BB)\par \par \uc2 1553 GMT [Dow Jones] Two- 5- and 10-year Tsy notes all in demand in Tsys O/N repo market Wed., trading lower than the GC rate. GC rate has averaged 0.27% Wed., says Scott Skyrm, a repo trader at Newedge, with 2-yrs between 0.12% and 0.06%. 2s and 5s have been popular last few days, close to 0, as investors take last grasps of the notes. New 2s and 5s settle Mon. and Skyrm doesn't expect them to continue trading special next week. Thurs. repo mkt could come under more pressure, he said, with GC seen trading between 0.30% and 0.35%, given large amount of cash management bills settling. (DLB)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 25, 2009 12:00 ET (17:00 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020090820e52p0pe4x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
