\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 3-Forint, zloty firm but  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  seen fragile\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sandor Peto and Dagmara Leszkowicz   \par \uc2 984 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2009\par 17:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, with new comments and prices.)\par \par \uc2 BUDAPEST/WARSAW, Feb 6 (Reuters) - Central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed on Friday, with the forint and the zloty firming due to planned \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures, even though Hungary published dismal industrial output data.\par \par \uc2 The Czech crown <EURCZK=> and the Romanian leu <EURRON=> stayed near Thursday's levels after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rate cuts in both countries earlier this week, just like Croatia's kuna <EURHRK=>, while the Serbian dinar <EURRSD=> eased.\par \par \uc2 Analyst forecasts in a Reuters poll showed that the region's main \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were expected to weaken in the next months and not expected to recover from the steep falls of the past months until the second half of the year. [ID:nL5740488]\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been hit by figures showing a drastic economic slowdown in the export-heavy region or recession in the case of Hungary, and because central banks have delivered rate cuts to help their economy weather the global crisis.\par \par \uc2 The latest in a string of bad economic figures was a 19.6 percent annual plunge in Hungary's industrial output in December according to figures published early on Friday. [ID:nL6235673]\par \par \uc2 Despite the figures, the forint extended gains posted on Thursday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced that it would launch a five billion euro facility of six-month swaps to help domestic banks maintain last year's levels in lending to companies.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said the news also helped the Polish zloty and later both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which have been tightly correlated in the past days, received additional support from comments about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [ID:nL662315]\par \par \uc2 The forint <EURHUF=> traded at 289.76 against the euro at 1542 GMT, firmer by 1.2 percent, while the zloty gained 1.23 percent to 4.556 per euro.\par \par \uc2 "Frankly, I don't think that it was the last time earlier this week that we saw the forint at 300," one dealer said. "Both the forint and the zloty are very vulnerable, they will react to any negative news and their weakening will not be slower than their current firming."\par \par \uc2 The Romanian leu was flat at 4.25 against the euro, while the Czech crown eased 0.14 percent to 27.985.\par \par \uc2 The crown gave up Thursday's one percent gains which it posted after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  surprised the market with a forecast showing it expected the average crown rate versus the euro at 25.8 this year, much stronger than current levels.\par \par \uc2 But dealers and analysts expect much weaker levels due to the declining econmy.\par \par \uc2 "This is an expected pullback for the crown," a Prague dealer said. "There is no reason to see the EURCZK at such low levels."\par \par \uc2 Czech Deputy Finance Minister Eduard Janota said on Friday that the country would not rush into its planned Eurobond issue because spreads were too high. [ID:NPRA002357]\par \par \uc2 Czech domestic bond yields ticked up and Hungarian government bonds also extended their losses, taking no encouragement from the forint's rebound which traders said was caused by profit-taking by foreign banks rather than inflows into the debt market.\par \par \uc2 "It's worrying that the forint recovery had no impact... this is not good news ahead of the planned restart of government bond auctions next week, but it's good to try, if the auctions fail, sentiment cannot become worse than now," one trader said.[ID:nL6582511]\par \par \uc2 The leu shrugged off \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments that Romania needs to keep its monetary policy tight until fiscal and wage restrictiveness is enforced [ID:nL6708438].\par \par \uc2 "Monetary policy depends on the fiscal policy and the fiscal policy is the biggest uncertainty so far," one dealer said. "Nobody knows how or if the budget deficit target of 2 percent of GDP will be achieved."\par \par \uc2 Dealers have said they suspected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  covertly in the market earlier this week as economic concerns and uncertainty over fiscal plans weighed on the leu. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ----------------------MARKET SNAPSHOT-------------------------\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                     Latest   Previous Local    Local\par                                    close    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par                                             change   change\par                                             today    in 2009 \par  Czech crown      <EURCZK=>  27.985   27.945   -0.14%    -4.4%\par  Polish zloty     <EURPLN=>   4.556    4.612   +1.23%    -9.68%\par  Hungarian forint <EURHUF=> 289.76   293.23    +1.2%     -9.05%\par  Croatian kuna    <EURHRK=>   7.396    7.4     +0.05%    -0.42%\par  Romanian leu     <EURRON=>   4.25     4.25     0%       -5.54%\par  Serbian dinar    <EURRSD=>  92.235   91.726   -0.55%    -2.99%\par  Yield Spreads\par  Czech treasury bonds <0#CZBMK=>\par  2-yr T-bond   CZ2YT=RR   +39 basis points to 