\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 2-East Europe  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  slide amid recession fears\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gareth Jones   \par \uc2 712 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2009\par 16:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds Polish PM on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , paragraph 12)\par \par \uc2 WARSAW, Feb 4 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  across Eastern Europe tumbled, Romania cut interest rates and a ratings agency warned of risks facing Hungary on Wednesday as the impact of global recession intensified in the region.\par \par \uc2 Poland, the European Union's largest former communist member, ruled out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rescue its plunging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for fear of squandering precious \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves at a time of economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 Protests flared again in the region as Bulgarian farmers blocked a Danube River border crossing with Romania, demanding subsidies and protection from cheap farm imports in a rally that mimicked protests in Greece.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors fleeing a region now perceived as too risky drove Poland's zloty <EURPLN=> down to 4.67 per euro, its lowest level since June 2004, just after the country joined the EU. Hungary's forint hit a record low of 304 per euro.\par \par \uc2 Investors fear that declining manufacturing output across the region, which is heavily dependent on exports to a euro zone now deep in recession, will hammer tax revenues and force spending cuts that will further squeeze demand in a vicious downward spiral.\par \par \uc2 Ratings agency \uc2 Moody's\uc2  said the gloomy outlook posed a risk to Hungary, which late last year clinched emergency loans from the International Monetary Fund and EU aid to avoid an economic meltdown.\par \par \uc2 "The negative outlook on Hungary and every other country indicates that the likely direction in the medium term is towards lower rating," Dietmar Hornung, senior analyst at Moody's Investors Service in Frankfurt, told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  is content for now with Budapest's rating, he added.\par \par \uc2 The heavily indebted economy of Hungary is expected to shrink by up to 3 percent in 2009.\par \par \uc2 Plunging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  compound the woes of Polish, Hungarian, Czech and other consumers saddled with loans denominated in euros, which in turn raises the risks to the region's mostly foreign-owned banks.\par \par \uc2 NO \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTIONS\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 , still among the region's stronger economies, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  head Slawomir Skrzypek, the economy minister and head of government all said they saw no reason for market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to underpin the zloty.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  today could have the opposite effect ... it would be using public money without a guarantee that it would work. Today, most experts advise not to do it," Prime Minister Donald Tusk said in an interview on TVN 24.\par \par \uc2 Poland's centre-right government unveiled plans on Tuesday to find savings worth 19.7 billion zlotys ($5.4 billion) in the state budget.\par \par \uc2 Defence Minister Bogdan Klich said on Wednesday Poland would withdraw its troops from peacekeeping missions in Chad and the Middle East in a decision expected to save 2 billion zlotys.\par \par \uc2 Polish economic growth is expected to slow this year to below 2 percent from 4.8 percent in 2008.\par \par \uc2 Slovakia's growth will drop sharply to 2.4 percent this year, below a previous forecast of 4.6 percent, cutting state revenues and boosting the fiscal deficit, Finance Minister Jan Pociatek said.\par \par \uc2 In Romania, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut interest rates by a quarter point to 10 percent, easing the cost of money for the first time in 19 months as cash shortages squeeze its economy.\par \par \uc2 Economists say Romania, which joined the EU along with southern neighbor Bulgaria in 2007, faces possible recession or even a financing crisis if the new government fails to convince investors it is serious about shoring up the state budget.\par \par \uc2 The Czech Republic, facing the possibility of zero growth this year, is also expected to cut rates on Thursday, following the example of Poland and Hungary last month. But cutting borrowing costs also piles further pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The worsening economic situation has recently stirred protests in Greece, Bulgaria, Latvia, Lithuania, Iceland and Russia. On Wednesday, Bulgarian farmers shut down the Danube bridge link with Romania to protest against falling farm prices.\par \par \uc2 Russia has been especially hard hit by the flight of foreign capital from global emerging markets. On Wednesday, the Fitch ratings agency downgraded Russia's sovereign debt. (Reporting by regional bureaux, writing by Gareth Jones, editing by Tom Hals)\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL-EASTERNEUROPE/ (WRAPUP 2)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|D|M|BNX|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090204e524001cx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
