\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Poland \highlight\uc2  should avoid euro vote,  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 -cbankers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gabriela Baczynska   \par \uc2 665 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 2009\par 08:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds Deputy PM Pawlak, paragraphs 8-9)\par \par \uc2 WARSAW, Jan 6 (Reuters) - Poland should avoid a referendum on adopting the euro, a central banker said on Tuesday, as the zloty gained back ground against the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and another official played down the chances of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The zloty has lost about 30 percent against the euro since last summer amid a general global investor flight from risk, supporting a push by Prime Minister Donald Tusk's cabinet for a swift change to the euro, in early 2012.\par \par \uc2 But many economists remain sceptical that it can meet this target and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymaker Andrzej Wojtyna said on Tuesday global financial gloom and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility may erode faith in the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the first public comment by a central banker on the referendum issue, Wojtyna, a moderate on the bank's 10-strong rate-setting Monetary Policy Council (MPC), said the zloty's volatility could sway Polish voters' views.\par \par \uc2 "Since the discussions before a referendum would take place in a time of crisis, we can expect high volatility in the zloty, which could in turn trigger significant changes in support for the euro," Wojtyna wrote in an article in Rzeczpospolita daily.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Tusk himself ruled out a referendum, though the eurosceptical main opposition party Law and Justice (PiS) has made such a vote the price of its support for necessary constitutional changes.\par \par \uc2 Wojtyna said it might be better to postpone resolving the euro issue until after the next parliamentary elections, due in the autumn of 2011, if the government and opposition Law and Justice (PiS) party fail to reach agreement on a euro timetable.\par \par \uc2 Later on Tuesday, Deputy Prime Minister Waldemar Pawlak reaffirmed the government's determination to adopt the euro.\par \par \uc2 "The time (of entry) is of smaller significance, it's the exchange-rate (that matters) and there is no doubt the current \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  level is much more beneficial," he told a news conference after a weekly cabinet meeting.\par \par \uc2 The zloty <EURPLN=>, which has shed some 30 percent since July on the back of the global turmoil, gained 1.7 percent against the euro on the day as global investors' appetite for risk improved at the start of the new year.\par \par \uc2 NO \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Slawomir Skrzypek in December said there might also be a need for the bank to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem the zloty's sharp depreciation, but his deputy Witold Kozinski played down such suggestions on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Kozinski said \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not need \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  now, though the MPC would consider such a move "if the zloty weakens significantly like 10-15 percent over a week".\par \par \uc2 Poland has had a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime since April 2000. The law says the government sets exchange rate policy with the cooperation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the bank can \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  threatens its inflation target.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been broadly supportive of government plans to adopt the euro in 2012, though Governor Skrzypek -- an ally of Law and Justice -- has criticised the strategy.\par \par \uc2 President Lech Kaczynski, twin brother of PiS leader Jaroslaw Kaczynski, reaffirmed on Tuesday his support for a referendum on the euro and said adopting the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2012 was "undoable".\par \par \uc2 Before entering the eurozone a country must keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inside the exchange rate mechanism or ERM-2 for at least two years.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's deputy governor criticised as premature Tusk's plan to put the zloty into ERM-2 in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 "When it comes to ERM-2, I think 2009 is not yet good enough," Kozinski told TVN CNBC, without elaborating.\par \par \uc2 He added that the advantages of euro adoption outweighed the disadvantages for Poland. (Additional reporting by Kuba Jaworowski) (Writing by Gareth Jones; editing by Patrick Graham)\par \par \uc2 POLAND-EURO/ (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|G|PX|RNP|DNP|PGE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090106e516000cn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
