\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 2-Czech crown, Polish zloty hit records, forint strong\b0\par\par\uc2 587 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 2008\par 11:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Adds updated records, Polish central banker comment)\par \par \uc2  PRAGUE, July 14 (Reuters) - The Czech crown and Polish zloty inched up to new highs on Monday, extending record gains over  the past month that have spread to other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped by interest rate hike expectations.\par \par \uc2  The crown <EURCXZK=> hit a record 23.280 against the euro, up from 23.425 at Friday's close. \par \par \uc2  The zloty <EURPLN=> firmed to a new high of 3.2540 to the euro in the afternoon before pulling back to 3.2565 after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation. The zloty closed at 3.258 per euro on Friday.\par \par \uc2  "It's a continuation of the trend from the past few weeks where we've seen strong outperformance from regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Martin Blum, head of emerging macroeconomic research at \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  in Vienna.\par \par \uc2  The region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have firmed in the past month to record highs as risk averse investors sought alternatives to usual high-return investments and the market bet central banks would further combat inflation with interest rate hikes.\par \par \uc2  Blum said the region has been boosted by investors shifting positions from what are perceived as more risky Asian assets.\par \par \uc2  "It's an ongoing reallocation," he said.\par \par \uc2  STAYING CLEAR\par \par \uc2  Central bankers have become increasingly concerned with the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength on economies that are already slowing due to lower demand from western trade partners.\par \par \uc2  However, that strength has also helped combat price growth, bringing about prospects for a pause in rate tightening, and policymakers have stopped short of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Slawomir Skrzypek said on Monday his bank did "not expect to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market".\par \par \uc2  The Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has added 3.5 percent against the euro in the past four weeks and 11.4 percent since Jan. 1. The zloty has firmed 3.8 percent and 9.8 percent in the same periods.\par \par \uc2  The Slovak crown, which will be switched for euros on January 1, 2009, has also gained 9.9 percent since the start of the year, and traders say its steep appreciation has brought regional neighbours' prospects for euro zone entry more to the forefront, boosting investor interest.\par \par \uc2  "It is sort of a knee-jerk reaction," one trader with a London bank said.\par \par \uc2  Hungary's forint <EURHUF=> has done better that the Czech and Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , strengthening 6.3 percent in the past month and 9.2 percent for the year. It traded near an all-time high of 229.55 to the euro on Monday.\par \par \uc2  The Romanian leu <EURRON=> is up 2.4 percent in the past month. It was at 3.57 per euro on Monday, near its 2008 high.\par \par \uc2  Analysts say there are still a chance for further interest rate rises in the region due to demand-led inflationary pressures. Data in the past week from the Czech Republic, Slovakia, Hungary and Romania showed inflation in June was nearing predicted peak levels but likely to remain elevated.\par \par \uc2  "The strength of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if it persists, will have an impact," \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  wrote in its weekly Emerging Weekly on Friday. "Does it mean that the rate hike cycle has come to an end in eastern Europe? We do not think so."\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Jason Hovet and Dagmara Leszkowicz; Editing by Ian Jones)  \par \par \uc2 EASTEUROPE-MARKETS/\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  (WRAPUP 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|CZS|PX|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080714e47e000or\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
