\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Candidates for Polish economic ministries  \b0\par\par\uc2 967 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2005\par 16:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 Sept 12 (Reuters) - Following are profiles of potential candidates for key economic portfolios under Poland's expected new coalition of the economically liberal Civic Platform (PO) party and conservatives Law and Justice (PiS).  \par \par \uc2 For a FACTBOX on the two parties economic policy programmes, click on: [ID:nL02669490].  \par \par \uc2 FINANCE MINISTER  \par \par \uc2 Barring a strong victory for Law and Justice, politicians and analysts widely expect the ministry to go to the Civic Platform and its agenda of fiscal cuts, fast euro entry and lower corporate and income tax rates.  \par \par \uc2 But the new minister is likely to face Law and Justice resistance to cuts in welfare benefits and a swift push into the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and will need to manage a compromise over the two parties' differing tax proposals.  \par \par \uc2 Political sources say the Civic Platform is as yet undecided whether to make a political appointment to the ministry or pick one of the economic experts who work on its policy programme.  \par \par \uc2 STEFAN KAWALEC (CIVIC PLATFORM)  \par \par \uc2 A respected economist and finance ministry veteran from the early 1990s, Kawalec, 52, keeps a low public profile and has avoided the political limelight.  \par \par \uc2 He began his post-communist career during much-praised "shock therapy" market reforms in 1989-1991, when he was chief adviser to then finance minister, and now \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, Leszek Balcerowicz.  \par \par \uc2 Later a deputy finance minister, he stayed on under the leftist government only to be sacked in 1994 in a controversy surrounding a major banking privatisation, which may make him less palatable for Law and Justice.  \par \par \uc2 Kawalec has worked in business since and is now director of strategy with Poland's biggest insurer PZU SA. He has led the Platform's economic policy working group for the last two years.  \par \par \uc2 HANNA GRONKIEWICZ-WALTZ (CIVIC PLATFORM)  \par \par \uc2 As \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor in 1992-2000, Gronkiewicz-Waltz, 53, oversaw reforms which nursed Poland's banking sector out of crisis, re-establishing lender faith and eventually helping make it the most streamlined and competitive in central Europe.  \par \par \uc2 A law lecturer, she was deputy head of the European Bank for Reconstruction and Development after leaving the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and remains untested on fiscal policy. She speaks English, Russian and French.  \par \par \uc2 "It is a very tough job and I think that the minister must be someone who is expert in budget affairs and close to the vision of (Platform prime ministerial candidate) Jan Rokita, who wants to drive economic matters," she told Reuters recently about the finance minister post.  \par \par \uc2 Well-connected politically, she leads the Platform's Warsaw electoral list, and is also cited as a potential candidate for mayor of the Polish capital.  \par \par \uc2 ZBIGNIEW CHLEBOWSKI (CIVIC PLATFORM)  \par \par \uc2 Chlebowski, 41, has been the Platform's main economic spokesman, although he has little practical experience with implementing economic policy at a national level.  \par \par \uc2 For 11 years the mayor of his home town in lower Silesia, he entered parliament at the last election and cut his policy teeth chairing a committee dealing with fiscal reform plans. If the party opts for a political appointee, sources say Chlebowski could lead the race along Gronkiewicz-Waltz.  \par \par \uc2 He has a degree from Wroclaw's Academy of Agriculture. He speaks fluent German and some English.  \par \par \uc2 RAFAL ANTCZAK (CIVIC PLATFORM)  \par \par \uc2 A die-hard liberal economist, Antczak, 35, works at the CASE research institute, seen as close to the views of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Leszek Balcerowicz, and is generally seen as the brains behind the Platform's economic policy programme.  \par \par \uc2 Sharp and uncompromising in arguing his views, he designed the party's flat 15 percent rate corporate, VAT and income tax policy and supports sharp cuts in welfare and administrative spending.  \par \par \uc2 Since 1994 he has worked as a policy adviser to governments and central banks in Ukraine, Kirgistan, Kazakhstan, Belarus and Moldova. He speaks fluent English, good Russian and some Ukrainian and Belarussian.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY/STATE \b \highlight22\uc2 TREASURY MINISTRY\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Political sources and analysts say a strong result for Law and Justice would lead it to fight hard for control of a new economy super-ministry which both parties want to replace the current economy and treasury ministries.  \par \par \uc2 The new ministry will be charged with speeding up privatisation as well as supporting growth and job creation.  \par \par \uc2 ADAM SZEJNFELD (CIVIC PLATFORM)  \par \par \uc2 The head of the lower house of parliament's Economy Committee and a lawyer by training, Szejnfeld. 46, has close ties with business and supports a wide-ranging rationalisation of state administration and taxation.  \par \par \uc2 In the last parliament he authored numerous bills aimed at encouraging entrepreneurship, investment and job creation, particularly among small and medium-sized businesses but has no experience of running economic policy in government.  \par \par \uc2 PIOTR KAMINSKI  \par \par \uc2 Currently a board member at Poland's state-controlled largest bank PKO BP, Kaminski, 36, is a market insider with experience as the deputy head of the Warsaw Stock Exchange and eight years at Poland's Securities Commission.  \par \par \uc2 He has a business degree from Warsaw University and is known to have good contacts in the Platform.  \par \par \uc2 CEZARY MECH (LAW AND JUSTICE)  \par \par \uc2 The author of Law and Justice's economic programme, Mech is an economist by training and the former head of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's pension fund watchdog. He favours an \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  economic policy, but also supports the fiscal reforms needed to stabilise public finances and ensure Poland meets euro entry conditions.  \par \par \uc2 In July's policy programme Mech proposed freezing the budget deficit at 30 billion zlotys in 2006-9, along with a series of tax rebates for business to spur job creation.  \par \par \uc2 But he also warned against rushing too fast into the euro, saying it would only benefit foreign speculators, and called for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to significantly lower Polish interest rates to weaken the zloty and boost credit growth.  \par \par \uc2 POLAND-MINISTERS (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|PX|ABN|M|D|BNX|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050912e19c001k7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
