\par\par \b \uc2 Polish zloty hits new high, bonds rebound.  \b0\par\par\uc2 489 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 15:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     WARSAW, March 1 (Reuters) - Poland's zloty hit a fresh\par 32-month high to the euro on Tuesday as foreign investors poured\par more capital into central Europe, betting strong growth and a\par raised investment profile will back more gains this year.\par     Bonds retreated as investors took some profit-taking on an\par almost 30 basis point fall in yields since the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's\par surprise jump to an easing policy bias last Friday.\par     Analysts say the zloty has gained in recent days chiefly on\par bond market flows, but dealers put Tuesday's gains against both\par the euro and dollar down to a combination of speculative and\par fund capital, possibly also meant for corporate purchases.\par     Current account data on Monday showed foreign funds poured\par 558 million euros into Warsaw stocks in December while the\par treasury on Tuesday invited offers for a power plant worth an\par estimated $230 million, one of a series of big privatisations\par planned for 2005.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "This is a trend that no-one is willing to oppose and\par everyone wants to take advantage of," said a dealer with one\par large Warsaw bank. "Gains should continue across the region and\par the nearest target is 3.85 to the euro."\par     The zloty has gained 23 percent against the euro since\par European Union entry last May, including 15 percent in the last\par six months, with prospects of interest rate cuts and political\par instability ahead of this year's election yet to weaken the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     On Tuesday it rose 0.7 percent against both the euro and\par dollar, also helped by predictions from shadow prime minister\par Jan Rokita of further gains when the next government moves\par forward on fiscal reform.\par     But analysts added Rokita's suggestion Poland could adopt\par the euro before joining the European Monetary Union to protect\par its economy from further zloty gains, showed the strong\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's cooling impact on growth may become an issue in the\par election campaign.\par     "At the moment we haven't heard too many complaints about\par zloty strength from the government, but if cyclical\par macro-economic indicators were to cool further, if won't be long\par before we do," said Koon Chow in London.\par     "There is a risk that ahead of the general elections \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par policy and the zloty will become a political football, leading\par to growing pressure on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Koon\par Chow emerging markets strategist at CSFB.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has stuck to its hands-off policy to\par the exchange rate, but says further zloty gains could help curb\par inflation and create room for monetary easing.\par  \par  ----------------- MARKET SNAPSHOT AT 1430 GMT -----------------\par  EUR/PLN  3.8769 vs 3.9003 at 0900 on Tuesday\par  USD/PLN  2.9400 vs 2.9604\par  2-yr yield (OK0407)  5.38 pct vs 5.36 pct\par  5-yr bond yield (DS0310)  5.42 pct vs 5.37 pct\par  10-yr bond yield (DS1015)  5.53 pct vs 5.47\par  ---------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050301e13100bkq\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb